1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,620
So, um, today we are focusing on module one, video one point three.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:10,680
And, you know, usually when you review a project draft in your ministry, you wanna see every single blank filled in.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:17,280
But, uh, we're exploring why a blank space is actually much safer than a number that just looks certain.

4
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:18,820
Right. Yeah.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:22,580
So in this deep dive, we're unpacking the SDD method.

6
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,160
That's, um, specification-driven development.

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:35,860
Yeah, and the mission here is to really see why guessing answers just to keep a program moving is a massive liability, and you know exactly how you can spot these hidden guesses in your own

8
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:37,980
documents before the system becomes,

9
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:40,120
well, costly to change.

10
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:45,940
Yeah. To really grasp the SDD method, I mean, we have to start with the philosophy.

11
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,760
The written document must be the absolute source of truth for the builder.

12
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:52,980
Like, if it isn't in the spec, it just, uh, it doesn't get built.

13
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:54,140
Right.

14
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:58,400
So let's test that logic with the Progressa scenario.

15
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:01,160
Progressa is a fictional country, um, just to be clear.

16
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:09,180
Right. Yeah. Entirely fictional. Yeah. So imagine you're evaluating a system for their Higher Education Quality Authority, PHEQA.

17
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,780
They need a portal for institutions to appeal decisions.

18
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:19,640
Uh-huh. The regulations guarantee an appeal right, but they actually, um, they leave out the specific time limit.

19
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,960
Yeah, which, you know, happens all the time in government programs.

20
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:29,780
Exactly. So the analyst needs a number just to draw the software screens, and they just guess thirty days.

21
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:35,180
So what is the actual fallout for the ministry when a portal like this goes live with a guessed number?

22
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:40,480
Well, the fallout is immediate and, uh, worse, it is completely invisible.

23
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:45,120
Because if the true legal appeal period is actually longer than thirty days,

24
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:46,500
the system will just

25
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:49,920
unlawfully reject valid legal appeals on day thirty-one.

26
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:52,660
Wow, just automatically locking them out.

27
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:56,440
Right. The core issue isn't merely that the guess was wrong.

28
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:59,000
It is that once a guess is written down,

29
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,000
nobody can distinguish it from a verified fact.

30
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:09,440
Right, because a blank space is, you know, highly visible to reviewers, but a guess gets built into the system as absolute truth.

31
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:14,740
Exactly. The system just becomes fragmented, siloed, duplicated,

32
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,560
and maybe even rekeyed across departments.

33
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,200
Oh, man, and locked into one supplier.

34
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:23,680
Precisely. It becomes incredibly costly to change.

35
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:30,700
Okay, but if I'm managing this project, and I hand my minister a specification document full of literal blank spaces,

36
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:32,800
they're gonna think I haven't done my job.

37
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,200
Yeah. I mean, it feels counterintuitive.

38
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:41,460
Right. Like, how do you justify leaving gaps without looking lazy while still keeping the project moving?

39
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:47,480
Well, you have to flip the process. You have to make what you don't know the main feature of the document.

40
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:50,860
Okay, so that's the SDD method in action.

41
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:58,900
Yes, exactly. Instead of sneaking in a guessed thirty days, the document has to explicitly state that the appeal period is

42
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:02,320
to be confirmed by the registrar of PHEQA.

43
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,560
Okay, so you transform a hidden flaw into a managed task.

44
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:11,380
You literally name the gap and count it, like, um, open question seven of nine.

45
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:12,180
Right.

46
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:19,440
That way, the builders can build the entire rest of the portal and just simply leave that one specific setting blank for the registrar.

47
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,180
Got it. So if I am the leader reviewing these drafts,

48
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:26,580
I need a way to catch these guesses before they reach the builders.

49
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:30,060
You do. And there is a specific manager's test.

50
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:34,180
It is three critical questions you have to ask before you sign off on anything.

51
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:35,340
Okay, what are they?

52
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:36,160
Number one:

53
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:42,320
Can someone build from this document alone without asking anything apart from the named open questions?

54
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,760
That definitely forces the writer to be entirely self-contained.

55
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:46,840
What's number two?

56
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:53,640
Number two: Was a review held with the document owner, the builder, and the official who will actually live with the result?

57
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:56,160
That's crucial. It breaks down the silos.

58
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:02,020
It forces the analyst who wrote the rule to sit face to face with the developer and the official who will use it.

59
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:04,320
Precisely. And number three:

60
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:08,660
Is every remaining gap named, owned, and counted?

61
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:10,840
Okay, so three questions first.

62
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:17,060
Now, when I am looking at a draft in my ministry, what is the exact mechanism for spotting a failure?

63
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:18,779
What is the red flag I should look for?

64
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:25,360
You scan the document specifically for figures, periods, roles, and rules with no source beside them.

65
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,000
So no citation at all.

66
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:27,540
Right.

67
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:33,920
Whenever you spot a number on its own, like thirty days, you always ask, "Where did this come from?"

68
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:35,160
Right. Where did it come from?

69
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:40,720
An honest answer is either a documented source or a specific person's name who owns the decision.

70
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:44,880
So if a draft lands on my desk with zero open questions,

71
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:49,980
that's not a success. That's a warning sign that someone just answered the hard questions alone.

72
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:51,000
Exactly.

73
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:54,620
A question with an owner belongs in a document you sign.

74
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:58,960
A guessed answer never does, even if that guess happens to be right.

75
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:07,000
Many of the foundational facts embedded in legacy systems today are actually just historical guesses that nobody ever bothered to question.
